\title{Composite}
\date{2026-04-13T21:00:38Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Definition}

\p{Integers #{> 1} that are not prime are called \em{composite}.}

\p{That is, a composite number #{n} is one which has at least one divisor #{d \mid n} with #{1 < d < n}.  Note that if #{d \mid n}, then by definition #{dk = n} for some integer #{k}, which means #{k} is also a divisor of #{n}; divisors come in pairs (except for the special case when #{n = d^2}).}

